  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Friday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: A large upper level ridge of high\npressure over the southwest CONUS continues to situate near the\nfour corners high. This pattern is keeping high temperatures\nacross the region 10-15 degrees above normal for this time of\nyear. Various heat related products are in effect across the entire \nregion as high temperatures remain in the upper 90s to 100s with \nModerate to Major heat risk indices across the area.\n\nTighter weather gradients to the north of Nevada will increase\nwesterly winds in the northern zones with speeds up to 15-20 mph,\ngusts as high as 20-25 mph. Elevated fire weather conditions will\nbe present tomorrow due to breezy winds along with low minimum \nrelative humidity and hot temperatures.\n\nTowards Central Nevada, high pressure over the four corners will \npush monsoonal moisture back into the region. PWAT values, increase \nin CAPE, and increased instability from high temperatures has parts \nof Central Nevada, in particular over White Pine County, will see \ndry thunderstorm development until the evening hours. These \nthunderstorms are expected to be short lived as storm motion is \nslow, which will result with thunderstorm collapsing on themselves \nas downdrafts will cut off the storm updrafts. Despite the short \nlived storms, the greatest threat is still the potential for dry \nlightning and strong gusty outflow winds from any thunderstorms \nreaching up near 45 mph. \n\nSunday, monsoonal moisture will continue to move north into Nevada \nfrom the upper ridge with chances for monsoon thunderstorms to \nspread across central Nevada and reach into parts of Northeastern \nNevada. Confidence remains low, but thunderstorms across Central \nNevada expected to be a mix of wet/dry, with little precipitation \nmaking it into the valleys.\n\nInto the next week, chances for monsoon thunderstorms each\nafternoon as the upper ridge is expected to stay over the four\ncorners and push moisture into Nevada. Much of the activity is \nexpected to stay over Central Nevada and into parts of Northeastern \nNevada. Temperatures are expected to be cooler with highs in the 90s \nand low temperatures reaching back down into the 50s. Extreme Heat \nWarning will still be in effect for eastern Elko County until 11 pm \nMonday as high temperatures stay in the upper 90s, however low \ntemperatures remain high in the upper 70s to 80s.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence continue for near \nrecord heat across Northern and Central Nevada this afternoon.\nModerate confidence of elevated fire weather conditions due to hot, \ndry, and breezy winds over Northern Nevada this weekend. Moderate \nconfidence of critical fire weather conditions in Central Nevada due \nto isolated to scattered dry thunderstorms this afternoon into this \nevening. No deviations from the NBM model output.",
